{
  "_note": "Common mistakes (/gramatica/erros-comuns): Portuguese words learners mix up and expressions they build wrongly. Each pair has two sides (a, b: the word, its part of speech or a note in English, a Portuguese example with <b>), `why` the difference in one sentence, `tr` its translation into the help languages (empty for now: the page shows nothing then), `check` a gap to try (`answer` = a or b; `options` when the words on the buttons are not the bare a/b words). `regel` per group is the folding rule (*…* = example, **…** = bold), `regelTr` its translation, `comic` the comic at the top (MascotComic). `wrong: true` on b = this isn't correct Portuguese (or isn't the standard in Portugal), and its `ex` is an English note, not an example. Meaning traps (false friends) are on /conhecimentos/falsos-amigos, not here. The part-of-speech cards on /gramatica/classes-de-palavras point to a pair here through `fehler`. European Portuguese (1990 spelling as used in Portugal); variety on every pair, a ptBR note where Brazil differs. Group ids stay: the styles use them. idioms-mistakes run (2026-11-20), own draft, not yet reviewed by a native speaker.",
  "reviewed": false,
  "comic": {
    "title": "{é} or {e}?",
